# kask registry guidelines

## Convergence checks in skill manifests

When writing the `input_mapping` for a convergence-check step (the step that references `shared/convergence-check`), the `primary_result` must incorporate the results of **all** substantive steps in the PDCA loop, not just the first step. A convergence check that only sees `step_1_result` will report convergence while later steps (provenance, conflict resolution, variety assessment, VSM mapping, gap analysis, etc.) may have unresolved findings.

Pattern to use:

```yaml
input_mapping:
  primary_result:
    step_1_output: "{{ step_1_result }}"
    step_2_output: "{{ step_2_result }}"
    step_3_output: "{{ step_3_result | default({}) }}"
  convergence_description: "all analysis phases are stable"
  include_blockers: true
```

Exception: if a skill has a single substantive step (e.g., `essentialist` runs one gate-flow template), `primary_result: "{{ step_1_result }}"` is correct. If a skill uses conditional steps with fallbacks (e.g., `refactor-architecture` uses `step_7_result | default(step_3_result)`), the fallback pattern is acceptable as long as it covers the last possible substantive result.

This rule exists because the convergence-gain bug was found in 3 skills (`pragmatic-semantics`, `pragmatic-cybernetics`, `tdd`) where convergence used only an early step's result, allowing the loop to converge while later analysis steps had unresolved findings.

## Config sub-structs must reject unknown fields

Every config sub-struct in `bundle/config.rs` (`ErrorHandlingConfig`, `BundleAuditConfig`, `BundleGasConfig`, `RjouleConfig`, `BundleLedgerConfig`, `ConvergenceConfig`) must use `#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]`, not just `#[serde(default)]`. Without `deny_unknown_fields`, extra sub-fields in manifests are silently accepted and discarded — an operator setting `on_capability_denied: escalate` gets no signal that the field was dropped. `ManifestFile` already has `deny_unknown_fields`; the gap is that the sub-structs it deserializes into do not.

This rule exists because 23 manifests had silently-dropped `error_handling` and `audit` sub-fields before `deny_unknown_fields` was added to `ErrorHandlingConfig` and `BundleAuditConfig`. The remaining sub-structs (`BundleGasConfig`, `RjouleConfig`, `BundleLedgerConfig`, `ConvergenceConfig`) were clean at audit time but need the same guard to prevent future drift.
